{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation:** when authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration MUST be in the mandatory input set.\n\n**Implementation:** `AssessmentSources::prepare` (src/authoring/inputs.rs, lines 65–252) carries a `requirements.examples.authoring_inputs` {impl} reference.\n- It looks the subject up among the non-term definitions, so named examples are accepted.\n- It always makes `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }` the first selector.\n- `source_input` resolves that selector through `InputSelector::resolve` (src/inputs.rs). `resolve` fills `text` from each matching declaration's `raw_lines`.\n- `collect_definitions` (src/evidence.rs) builds `raw_lines` from the whole block span, `block.start..block.end`, so the opening `:::{example}` fence, the body and the closing `:::` are all included. Only option lines such as `:test:` are stripped. Those are covered separately, because anchors become outline-selector inputs in `prepare`.\n- The first selector is pushed to `mandatory` unless it is generated evidence, and a requirements selector never is. `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs` is cited as the contribution mechanism and is not reassessed here.\n\n**Test:** `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` carries a `{test}` reference to the subject.\n- It prepares sources for the example `requirements.case`.\n- It asserts that `subject_kind` is `Definition(Example, false)`.\n- It asserts that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the example's declaration is the first mandatory input.\n- It also asserts, from the requirement `requirements.ready`'s side, that the example (which cites and tests `requirements.ready`) appears among that requirement's inputs.\n- The test does not assert the fenced text of the example itself. That completeness is shared with requirements and is verified by `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` in src/evidence.rs, which asserts that the resolved text equals the fenced `raw_lines`. I judged this sufficient.\n\n**Marking and unspecified behavior:** I found no unmarked implementation or tests for this obligation. I found no extra behavior beyond the text, and no FIXME applies.",
  "references": "b899630e41f440f0f3cdb55b167fbf32959bdbf515b06e0008b680021fa1d4d4",
